### auth_lab.http — Authentication Gauntlet Lab
### Run a block with Ctrl+Alt+R (Windows/Linux) or Cmd+Alt+R (macOS).
### All endpoints live under http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/
### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


### ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
### PHASE 1 — Basic Authentication
### ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

### Phase 1 — Basic Auth (unauthenticated — should return 401)
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/basic/ HTTP/1.1

###

### Phase 1 — Basic Auth (with credentials — admin:admin123 in Base64)
# REST Client sends the raw Authorization header exactly as written below.
# YWRtaW46YWRtaW4xMjM= is Base64("admin:admin123")
# Check your terminal after sending — the server prints the raw header.
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/basic/ HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Basic YWRtaW46YWRtaW4xMjM=

###

### Phase 1 — Synthesis: Replay Attack (no password needed)
# An attacker captured this exact header off the network (plain HTTP = no TLS).
# They do NOT need to decode it — they simply copy and replay the same header.
# This works because Base64 is reversible ENCODING, not ENCRYPTION.
# The server cannot tell this apart from the real client's request.
# Lesson: Base64 provides ZERO security — TLS (HTTPS) is the only real shield.
GET http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/basic/ HTTP/1.1
Authorization: Basic YWRtaW46YWRtaW4xMjM=


